\ A parameter whose call sites disagree stays opaque, so its arithmetic keeps the
\ polymorphic path and the locked build is rejected. This is the negative control
\ for `param_type_inference_fib_strict.1z`.
\
\ `twice` is called once with a fixnum and once with a float, so agree-only
\ narrowing proves nothing about `x`. `dup +` then compiles with both operands
\ opaque, which emits one per-operation native fallback for the cold arm shared
\ by non-numeric operands and fixnum overflow, and any fallback rejects a locked
\ build.
\
\ `param_type_inference_narrowing.1z` covers the same word without the lock,
\ where the pass cannot type a fixnum arithmetic result at all. Under the lock it
\ can, so this is the one configuration where a missing conflict check would have
\ narrowed `x` to whichever call site was seen last.
\
\ Build-only: the golden is the rejection report.

twice: ( x -- y ) [ dup + ] ;

5 twice drop
2.5 twice drop
